{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>You shall not eat any abomination</b>. Behold, He wished to add an explanation concerning the forbidden foods, and therefore He said <b>לֹא תֹאכַל כָּל תּוֹעֵבָה</b>, to declare that all those forbidden are abominable to the pure soul. And the meaning of every תִּעוּב is hatred and repulsion, as in the verse כִּי נִתְעַב דְּבַר הַמֶּלֶךְ (I Chronicles 21:6), כָּל אֹכֶל תְּתַעֵב נַפְשָׁם (Psalms 107:18), for the forbidden foods are coarse and produce thickness and dullness in the soul, as I mentioned in its place (Exodus 22:30). And therefore He further said (Deuteronomy 14:20) כָּל עוֹף טָהוֹר תֹּאכֵלוּ, to declare that those which He forbade are abominable, while the rest are pure and fitting for a pure soul.\n\nAnd in the Sifrei they said: Rabbi Shimon says, כָּל עוֹף טָהוֹר תֹּאכֵלוּ — these are the clean locusts. וְכֹל שֶׁרֶץ הָעוֹף (Deuteronomy 14:19) — these are the unclean locusts. And well did he speak.\n\nNow the reason of the verse is that He mentioned prohibiting כָּל שֶׁרֶץ הָעוֹף, which are those that go on four, and then He returned and said כָּל עוֹף טָהוֹר תֹּאכֵלוּ, to permit from among them those that have knees above their legs — the אַרְבֶּה, the סָלְעָם, the חַרְגּוֹל, and the חָגָב — and there was no need to specify these since they had already been mentioned (Leviticus 11:21–22). But He included them by way of brevity: כָּל עוֹף טָהוֹר תֹּאכֵלוּ, meaning: these that are mentioned are abominable foods, while the rest of the fowl, each according to its kind, for the most part you may eat of them every clean one — which I have not forbidden.\n\nNow He generalized all those forbidden to us, that they are all תּוֹעֵבָה, and there was no need to detail after this the שְׁרָצִים and all the רֶמֶשׂ, for it is a known thing that the soul of every person clean of mind abhors them. But He mentioned the signs of the בְּהֵמָה and the חַיָּה and the fish and the fowl and the נְבֵלָה, to inform you that they too are abominable to the soul. And He did not mention the טְרֵפָה, for it is not תּוֹעֵבָה, but it was forbidden because of the venom or the fatal illness, so that it not harm those who eat it.\n\nAnd for our Sages there are further midrashic interpretations: why was the section repeated? For the בְּהֵמָה, because of the שְׁסוּעָה (the split animal); and for the fowl, because of the רָאָה (appearance) (Hullin 63a). And a further midrash (Sifrei 98; Kiddushin 57a): כָּל צִפּוֹר טְהֹרָה תֹּאכֵלוּ — to permit the dispatched bird of the מְצוֹרָע. And וְזֶה אֲשֶׁר לֹא תֹאכְלוּ מֵהֶם — to prohibit the שְׁחוּטָה. They received as follows: in <b>כָּל צִפּוֹר טְהֹרָה</b> there is a hint that a bird that has not received impurity is permitted, and the impurity of the מְצֹרָע does not adhere to a חַיָּה, for living creatures among the בְּהֵמָה and the fowl do not receive impurity when they touch a corpse or a שֶׁרֶץ. But the שְׁחוּטָה, which is fit to receive impurity, the spirit of impurity that flies forth from the מְצֹרָע will adhere to it, and it is not טְהוֹרָה, and it falls under the category of תּוֹעֵבָה, for eating it is abominable."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "Sifrei citation gives צח, likely a typo or variant for צח (~98); the same halakhah appears in Sifrei Devarim 98 and Kiddushin 57a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:50:31.106891452Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}